## What a distribution tells you

A **distribution** describes which values are common, which are rare,
and how spread out they are — where the **middle** is, how **wide** the
spread is, whether it **leans**.

::: callout-note
Name the shape and you get a lot for free: a way to summarise it in a
couple of numbers, and a way to judge whether any observation is
surprising.
:::

Three shapes cover most People Analytics data: **Normal**, **Binomial**,
**Poisson**.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The Normal distribution

### Why the bell curve appears *everywhere*

Because most things are pushed around by **many small, independent
influences that add up**. A salesperson's total sales are nudged by
territory, client mix, timing of deals, effort, luck… No single factor
dominates.

::: callout-important
Add lots of little nudges together and you almost always get a bell.
:::

You already meet Normals every day:

| **In daily life** | **In People Analytics** |
|---|---|
| Adult heights | Sales figures |
| Exam marks in a big cohort | Time to fill a role |
| Blood pressure readings | Daily applications received |
| Errors in careful measurement | Time to productivity for new hires |

### See it happen

Twelve small random nudges, added up:

```{r normal-emerges}
tibble(total = replicate(5000, sum(runif(12, -1, 1)))) |>
  ggplot(aes(total)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 45, fill = "#122a52", colour = "white") +
  labs(title = "A bell emerges from nothing",
       subtitle = "Nothing here is 'normal' by design — it simply appears",
       x = "Sum of 12 random nudges", y = "Count")
```

### The anatomy of a Normal

A Normal distribution is fully described by just **two numbers**: its
mean (where the hump sits) and its SD (how wide it is).

```{r normal-anatomy}
x <- seq(-4, 4, length.out = 400)
bind_rows(tibble(x, y = dnorm(x, 0, 1),   sd = "SD = 1"),
          tibble(x, y = dnorm(x, 0, 1.8), sd = "SD = 1.8")) |>
  ggplot(aes(x, y, colour = sd)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1.1) +
  scale_colour_manual(values = c("#122a52", "#d32f2f")) +
  labs(title = "Same centre, different width",
       subtitle = "The mean sets the centre; the SD sets the width. That's all.",
       x = NULL, y = "Density", colour = NULL)
```

### The 68–95 rule

```{r rule-68-95}
d <- tibble(x = seq(-4, 4, length.out = 400), y = dnorm(x))
ggplot(d, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_area(data = filter(d, x >= -2, x <= 2), fill = "#8fabd0", alpha = 0.7) +
  geom_area(data = filter(d, x >= -1, x <= 1), fill = "#122a52") +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  labs(title = "The 68–95 rule",
       subtitle = "≈68% within 1 SD (navy) · ≈95% within 2 SD (light)",
       x = "Standard deviations from the mean", y = "Density")
```

### Now our own data

```{r sales-raw}
ggplot(salespeople, aes(x = sales)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 30, fill = "#122a52", colour = "white") +
  labs(title = "Sales across the sales team",
       subtitle = "Does this look like the bell we've been drawing?",
       x = "Sales (thousands of dollars)", y = "Number of salespeople")
```

### Does a Normal actually fit?

```{r sales-normal-overlay}
m <- mean(salespeople$sales)
s <- sd(salespeople$sales)

ggplot(salespeople, aes(x = sales)) +
  geom_histogram(aes(y = after_stat(density)),
                 bins = 30, fill = "#122a52", colour = "white", alpha = 0.85) +
  stat_function(fun = dnorm, args = list(mean = m, sd = s),
                colour = "#d32f2f", linewidth = 1.1) +
  labs(title = "Sales with a Normal curve laid over it",
       subtitle = paste0("Normal with mean ", round(m), " and SD ", round(s)),
       x = "Sales (thousands of dollars)", y = "Density")
```

::: callout-note
The Normal describes the middle of the data reasonably well and may
miss a little in the tails — that's fine. A distribution is a **useful
simplification**, not a claim that reality is exactly this shape.
:::

