## The Poisson distribution

### Counting events in a window

Sometimes there's no natural *n* — events just happen at some average
rate: complaints per month, transfer requests per team, applications
per posting. The Poisson needs only **one** number: the rate, **λ**
(lambda).

### Our data: transfer requests

We'll get properly acquainted with the `managers` dataset in Chapter 8
— for now, a first look at `transfers`, the number of transfer requests
filed by a manager's team:

```{r poisson-fit}
lambda <- mean(managers$transfers, na.rm = TRUE)

managers |>
  count(transfers) |>
  mutate(observed = n / sum(n),
         poisson = dpois(transfers, lambda)) |>
  pivot_longer(c(observed, poisson), names_to = "source", values_to = "prob") |>
  ggplot(aes(x = transfers, y = prob, fill = source)) +
  geom_col(position = "dodge") +
  scale_fill_manual(values = c("#122a52", "#d32f2f")) +
  labs(title = "Transfer requests vs a Poisson distribution",
       subtitle = paste0("Poisson rate (lambda) = ", round(lambda, 2)),
       x = "Number of transfer requests", y = "Probability", fill = NULL)
```

::: callout-important
A distribution is a **model** — a useful approximation, never the
literal truth. The skill is knowing when the approximation is good
enough — and Chapter 13 comes back to this exact variable to show what
happens when you don't account for how much opportunity each manager's
team size gave for a transfer request to occur.
:::

