## Population vs sample

### You never see the whole picture

-   The **population** is everyone you care about — every employee,
    every candidate, ever
-   The **sample** is the handful you actually measured

::: callout-important
You compute everything from the sample, but want to say something
about the population. **That gap is where statistics lives.**
:::

### Today we'll cheat

We'll treat all `r nrow(salespeople)` salespeople as if they were the
whole **population**, so we know the "true" average sales figure:

```{r population-mean}
population_mean_sales <- mean(salespeople$sales)
population_mean_sales
```

### Take a sample — then take another

```{r repeated-samples}
mean(slice_sample(salespeople, n = 50)$sales)
mean(slice_sample(salespeople, n = 50)$sales)
mean(slice_sample(salespeople, n = 50)$sales)
```

Three analysts, three answers — and none of them wrong.

::: callout-note
**This is the 3.2 vs 2.9 puzzle from the start of the chapter.**
:::

