## The idea of a model

### Signal and noise

Every observation can be thought of as:

$$
\text{observation} = \text{signal} + \text{noise}
$$

-   The **signal** is the systematic part we care about — the true
    average sales figure, the true promotion rate, the real effect
-   The **noise** is everything else — the random wobble that makes
    each salesperson, and each sample, different

::: callout-note
A **model** says: "the signal looks like this, the noise looks like
that." Every distribution today is a candidate description of the
**noise**.
:::

### Where this is heading

Frequentist statistics answers *"how much would my estimate wobble if
I repeated the study?"*

Bayesian statistics answers the more direct question:

> *"Given the one sample I actually have, which values of the signal
> are plausible — and how sure am I?"*

Next chapter we start answering it.

